{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01HfR5X277nWRUdouxNEy4hd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018fPs869eCTwa8C4BBVrD7G","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5598/docs/constitutions/go-style.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2874,"cache_read_input_tokens":24770,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2874,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CxWPahfEDXRg9WhSaGA74R","session_id":"e97c73f4-4027-474d-b337-bb9a89ad4527","uuid":"a5754333-1f2b-4f70-8377-6bda1fb939d0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018fPs869eCTwa8C4BBVrD7G","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n2\t# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n3\t#\n4\t# Go Style Constitution\n5\t#\n6\t# This constitution defines Go coding standards and design patterns for the\n7\t# project. It is injected into the stitch prompt alongside the execution\n8\t# constitution so Claude knows how to write idiomatic, maintainable Go code.\n9\t\n10\tcopyright_header: |\n11\t  Every Go file must start with the SPDX copyright header before the package\n12\t  declaration:\n13\t\n14\t    // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n15\t    // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n16\t\n17\t  This applies to hand-written files (magefiles/) and generated files (cmd/,\n18\t  pkg/, internal/, tests/). The header must appear on lines 1-2, followed by a\n19\t  blank line, then the package clause.\n20\t\n21\t\n22\tduplication: |\n23\t  Before writing a function, search for existing code that does the same thing.\n24\t  Before adding a field, check whether a struct already carries it. When two\n25\t  pieces of code share logic, extract the common part. Use struct embedding to\n26\t  share fields. Use helper functions to share behavior. Use interfaces to share\n27\t  contracts. The threshold is two: if you write the same thing twice, extract it.\n28\t\n29\timplementation_strategy: |\n30\t  Write all source files before running any build or test commands. Plan the\n31\t  full implementation (types, functions, imports) mentally, then write each\n32\t  file in dependency order. Only run `go build` or `go vet` after all files\n33\t  are written. This minimizes turns and avoids incremental compile-fix loops\n34\t  that re-send the entire prompt context on each iteration.\n35\t\n36\t  When writing a new cmd/ utility:\n37\t  1. Read the SRD and existing pkg/ contracts.\n38\t  2. Write main.go with all flag parsing, logic, and error handling in one pass.\n39\t  3. Write the test file with all test cases in one pass.\n40\t  4. Run `go vet` once to verify compilation.\n41\t  5. Run `go test` once to verify behavior.\n42\t  6. Fix any issues in a single edit pass.\n43\t\n44\t  Common Go compilation errors to avoid on first write:\n45\t  - Missing imports: always include fmt, os, io, strings when doing I/O.\n46\t  - Unused imports: do not import packages speculatively.\n47\t  - Type mismatches: check pkg/ contract signatures before calling.\n48\t  - Missing error handling: every function that returns error must be checked.\n49\t  - Unused variables: do not declare variables before they are needed.\n50\t\n51\tdesign_patterns:\n52\t  - name: Strategy\n53\t    description: |\n54\t      Use when multiple implementations of the same operation exist and the\n55\t      caller picks one. Define a small interface. Each implementation is a struct\n56\t      (or a function type) satisfying that interface. The caller selects at\n57\t      configuration time, not with if chains.\n58\t    symptoms: |\n59\t      A switch or if/else ladder choosing between behaviors, a function parameter\n60\t      named mode or kind, boolean flags that toggle logic branches.\n61\t\n62\t  - name: Command\n63\t    description: |\n64\t      Use when an action needs to be stored, queued, undone, or composed. Each\n65\t      command is a struct with an Execute() method. Commands can carry undo logic,\n66\t      be chained into sequences, or be logged for replay.\n67\t    symptoms: |\n68\t      Inline exec.Command calls scattered across a function, repeated sequences of\n69\t      shell operations that differ only in arguments, actions that should be\n70\t      retryable or reversible.\n71\t\n72\t  - name: Facade\n73\t    description: |\n74\t      Use when a caller should not know about the internal steps of a multi-step\n75\t      process. The facade exposes one method; internally it orchestrates several\n76\t      components. Do not add toggle parameters to a facade. If the caller needs to\n77\t      control sub-steps, they should use the components directly.\n78\t    symptoms: |\n79\t      A function that grew boolean parameters to skip internal steps, a caller that\n80\t      must call three functions in a specific order, an Init/Do/Cleanup pattern that\n81\t      keeps getting duplicated.\n82\t\n83\t  - name: Factory\n84\t    description: |\n85\t      Use NewXxx() functions to construct objects. Return concrete types but accept\n86\t      interfaces as dependencies. Factories centralize validation and wiring so\n87\t      callers never construct partially initialized structs.\n88\t\n89\t  - name: Decorator\n90\t    description: |\n91\t      Use to add cross-cutting concerns (logging, timing, retries) without\n92\t      modifying the wrapped object. In Go, the decorator accepts and returns the\n93\t      same interface. HTTP middleware is the canonical example.\n94\t    symptoms: |\n95\t      Logging or timing code copy-pasted around every call to an interface, optional\n96\t      behavior toggled by a boolean that wraps the real logic.\n97\t\n98\t  - name: Builder\n99\t    description: |\n100\t      Use when an object has many optional fields and constructing it in one call is\n101\t      unwieldy. Prefer the Functional Options variant (WithXxx functions) for\n102\t      idiomatic Go over a mutable builder struct.\n103\t\n104\t  - name: Adapter\n105\t    description: |\n106\t      Use when existing code does not satisfy the interface a consumer expects.\n107\t      Write a thin wrapper that translates the foreign API into the local interface.\n108\t      Do not modify the foreign code.\n109\t\n110\t  - name: Observer\n111\t    description: |\n112\t      Use channels for asynchronous event notification between goroutines. One\n113\t      producer writes to a channel; multiple consumers listen. Prefer this over\n114\t      callback registration when the producer should not know about its consumers.\n115\t\n116\tinterfaces: |\n117\t  Every boundary between a parent package and its internal sub-packages must be\n118\t  defined by an interface. The parent package declares the interface; the internal\n119\t  sub-package provides a concrete type that satisfies it. This applies even when\n120\t  only one implementation exists today. The purpose is testability and explicit\n121\t  contracts at every package boundary, not speculative abstraction.\n122\t\n123\t  Accept interfaces as parameters; return concrete structs. Keep interfaces small:\n124\t  one to three methods. A large interface is a sign that the abstraction is wrong.\n125\t  Split it into focused interfaces and compose them.\n126\t\n127\t  Do not use interface{} or any as function parameters, return types, or struct\n128\t  fields. Every value must have a concrete type or a named interface. If a\n129\t  function needs to accept multiple types, define an interface that captures the\n130\t  shared behavior, or use generics with type constraints. Type assertions and\n131\t  type switches on interface{}/any are symptoms of a missing abstraction. The\n132\t  only acceptable uses are stdlib boundaries that require it (e.g., json.Marshal,\n133\t  fmt.Sprintf variadic args).\n134\t\n135\tstruct_and_function_design: |\n136\t  Each struct represents one concept. Each function does one thing. If a function\n137\t  takes more than three parameters, group related parameters into a config struct.\n138\t  If a function exceeds 200 lines, find a seam and split it. Name structs and\n139\t  functions by what they represent or do, not by how they are called. Avoid\n140\t  generic names (Manager, Handler, Helper, Processor) unless the struct genuinely\n141\t  manages, handles, or processes a well-defined resource.\n142\t\n143\treceiver_conventions: |\n144\t  Use a pointer receiver when the method mutates the receiver, when the struct\n145\t  contains a mutex or sync primitive, or when the struct is large enough that\n146\t  copying it on every call is wasteful. Use a value receiver for small, immutable\n147\t  types where copying is cheap and the method does not modify the value. All\n148\t  methods on a given type must use the same receiver kind — do not mix pointer\n149\t  and value receivers on one type.\n150\t\n151\t  Name receivers with a one- or two-letter abbreviation of the type in lowercase:\n152\t  o for Orchestrator, c for Config, s for Server. Never use self or this. The\n153\t  receiver name must be consistent across all methods of the type.\n154\t\n155\terror_handling: |\n156\t  Handle errors at the point they occur. Use guard clauses: check err != nil and\n157\t  return early so the main logic stays at minimal indentation. Wrap errors with\n158\t  context: fmt.Errorf(\"doing X: %w\", err). Each wrap adds the \"why\" at that\n159\t  layer, producing a readable chain when the error surfaces. Never silently\n160\t  discard an error with _ unless the operation is best-effort cleanup (e.g.,\n161\t  removing a temp file after the real work succeeded). When discarding, leave a\n162\t  comment explaining why.\n163\t\n164\tpanic_vs_error: |\n165\t  Return errors from all functions where the failure is a runtime condition —\n166\t  missing files, bad user input, network failures, external API errors. Use panic\n167\t  only for violations of a programming contract: a nil argument that must not be\n168\t  nil by the function's documented precondition, an enum value that cannot exist,\n169\t  an internal state that indicates a bug in the caller. Never panic on I/O\n170\t  failures or user-provided data. A panic that can be triggered by an external\n171\t  input is a bug.\n172\t\n173\tinit_functions: |\n174\t  Avoid init(). Every init() runs silently at program start, in dependency order,\n175\t  with no way for the caller to handle errors or control timing. Acceptable uses\n176\t  are registering stdlib drivers (e.g., _ \"github.com/mattn/go-sqlite3\") and\n177\t  initializing package-level read-only lookup tables that cannot fail. If\n178\t  initialization requires I/O or can return an error, use an explicit function\n179\t  called from main() or from a constructor, not init().\n180\t\n181\tno_magic_strings: |\n182\t  Centralize all string literals that name external binaries, file paths, URLs,\n183\t  or repeated text. Binary names: const (e.g., binGit, binGo). Paths, prefixes,\n184\t  module names: const. Shared CLI arg slices: var. Large prompts with variable\n185\t  interpolation: embedded .tmpl templates. Short messages with interpolation:\n186\t  fmt.Sprintf at the call site. Static messages or labels: const. When adding a\n187\t  new external command or path, define the constant first, then use it. Never\n188\t  scatter raw string literals across files.\n189\t\n190\tconfiguration_via_yaml: |\n191\t  All configuration must flow through configuration.yaml, loaded into the Config\n192\t  struct by LoadConfig(). Do not read os.Getenv() to control orchestrator\n193\t  behaviour, select execution modes, set file paths, or pass any option that a\n194\t  user would otherwise set in configuration.yaml. Environment variables are not\n195\t  a configuration channel in this project.\n196\t\n197\t  os.Getenv() is permitted only for two purposes: reading platform context\n198\t  provided by the operating system (HOME, PATH, TMPDIR) and, in test code only,\n199\t  reading variables that the test harness itself sets to override behaviour for\n200\t  the test process. In both cases, the env var must be documented at the call\n201\t  site with a comment explaining why it cannot come from Config.\n202\t\n203\t  The concrete consequence: if you find yourself writing os.Getenv(\"SOME_MODE\")\n204\t  or os.LookupEnv(\"SOME_FLAG\") outside of those two narrow contexts, stop and\n205\t  add a field to the relevant Config sub-struct instead.\n206\t\n207\tconstants: |\n208\t  Prefer typed constants over bare string or integer literals for values that\n209\t  form a fixed set. Give the type a name: type totalMode int, then define the\n210\t  values with iota in a const block. Use iota for sequential integer enums;\n211\t  do not assign explicit integers unless the values must match an external\n212\t  protocol. Group related constants in a single const block. Keep constants\n213\t  unexported unless callers outside the package need them. Program names, file\n214\t  paths, CLI flag names, label strings, and any literal that appears more than\n215\t  once are candidates for a named constant.\n216\t\n217\tproject_structure: |\n218\t  cmd/: Entry points. Minimal: parse flags, wire dependencies, start.\n219\t  internal/: Private implementation. Not importable outside this module. One\n220\t  package per component.\n221\t  pkg/: Public API surface. Exports types and delegates to internal sub-packages.\n222\t  tests/: Integration tests.\n223\t  magefiles/: Build tooling. Flat directory (mage constraint). One file per\n224\t  concern.\n225\t\n226\t  Every package organizes its implementation into internal/ sub-packages grouped\n227\t  by domain concept. The parent package is the public API surface: it exports\n228\t  types, defines interfaces, and delegates to internal sub-packages that provide\n229\t  the implementation. Internal sub-packages are not importable outside the parent,\n230\t  enforced by Go's internal/ directory convention. This rule applies uniformly to\n231\t  packages under pkg/ and to top-level internal/ packages alike.\n232\t\n233\t  Align package structure to SRD component structure. Each major component maps\n234\t  to one package. Avoid package names like util, common, helpers. Name packages\n235\t  by domain: storage, auth, config.\n236\t\n237\tfile_organization: |\n238\t  Name files by the concern or feature they implement: scaffold.go, stitch.go,\n239\t  analyze.go. Do not name files by kind: types.go, helpers.go, utils.go are\n240\t  forbidden names. Place tests in <file>_test.go alongside the file they test.\n241\t\n242\t  Within a file, order declarations as follows: package-level constants and\n243\t  types first, then constructors (NewXxx), then exported methods and functions,\n244\t  then unexported helpers. main() goes last in cmd/ entry points. This ordering\n245\t  means a reader can scan from top to bottom and encounter definitions before\n246\t  uses.\n247\t\n248\timport_organization: |\n249\t  Group imports into three blocks separated by blank lines, in this order:\n250\t  standard library, external modules, internal packages. Within each block,\n251\t  goimports ordering applies (alphabetical). Never use dot imports (. \"pkg\")\n252\t  except in test files where the convention is established for a specific\n253\t  package. Never use blank imports except for side-effect registration\n254\t  (database drivers, image format codecs).\n255\t\n256\t  Correct example:\n257\t    import (\n258\t      \"fmt\"\n259\t      \"os\"\n260\t\n261\t      \"github.com/spf13/cobra\"\n262\t      \"gopkg.in/yaml.v3\"\n263\t\n264\t      \"github.com/myorg/myrepo/internal/config\"\n265\t    )\n266\t\n267\tstandard_packages:\n268\t  - \"Build automation: magefile/mage\"\n269\t  - \"CLI framework: spf13/cobra\"\n270\t  - \"Configuration: spf13/viper\"\n271\t  - \"Observability: go.opentelemetry.io/otel\"\n272\t  - \"Testing assertions: stretchr/testify\"\n273\t  - \"SQL database access: database/sql (stdlib) + mattn/go-sqlite3\"\n274\t  - \"HTTP routing: net/http (stdlib) or chi\"\n275\t  - \"YAML parsing: gopkg.in/yaml.v3\"\n276\t  - \"JSON handling: encoding/json (stdlib)\"\n277\t  - \"UUID generation: google/uuid\"\n278\t\n279\tstruct_embedding: |\n280\t  When two or more consumers share configuration fields, extract a common struct\n281\t  and embed it. Provide a registerXxxFlags helper when the shared fields map to\n282\t  CLI flags. Do not duplicate fields across sibling structs.\n283\t\n284\tnaming_conventions:\n285\t  - \"Exported types and functions: PascalCase (e.g., CupboardConfig)\"\n286\t  - \"Unexported types and functions: camelCase (e.g., cobblerConfig)\"\n287\t  - \"CLI flags: kebab-case (e.g., --silence-agent)\"\n288\t  - \"Constants for binaries: bin prefix + PascalCase (e.g., binGit, binClaude)\"\n289\t  - \"Factory functions: New prefix (e.g., NewBackend())\"\n290\t  - \"Interface names: Action or capability (e.g., Table, Reader)\"\n291\t  - \"Receiver names: one- or two-letter abbreviation of the type (e.g., o for Orchestrator, c for Config)\"\n292\t  - \"Test helpers: verb-named functions describing what they do (e.g., buildFixture, writeFile, skipIfMissing)\"\n293\t  - \"Typed enum types: singular noun (e.g., type totalMode int, type blockUnit int)\"\n294\t\n295\tcomment_style: |\n296\t  Every exported symbol — function, type, variable, constant — must have a godoc\n297\t  comment. The comment begins with the name of the symbol: // Orchestrator holds\n298\t  the configuration and drives all build targets. Package-level documentation\n299\t  goes in a doc comment immediately above the package clause. Do not create a\n300\t  separate doc.go unless the package is too large to document inline.\n301\t\n302\t  Unexported functions need a comment only when the logic is non-obvious; a\n303\t  function named buildMeasurePrompt does not need a comment saying \"builds the\n304\t  measure prompt.\" Avoid comments that restate what the code clearly shows.\n305\t  Write comments that explain why, not what. When discarding an error, the\n306\t  comment must explain the reasoning: // best-effort cleanup, error ignored.\n307\t\n308\t  Reference requirement IDs in comments when implementing a specific requirement:\n309\t  // R1.2: truncate output at maxLines to bound memory usage.\n310\t\n311\tconcurrency: |\n312\t  Pass context.Context as the first parameter to any function that does I/O or\n313\t  may block. Never start a goroutine without a plan for how it exits. Use\n314\t  sync.WaitGroup or a done channel to manage lifetimes. Protect package-level\n315\t  mutable state (global variables modified at runtime) with a sync.Mutex or\n316\t  sync.RWMutex. Document that a variable requires locking and provide paired\n317\t  getter/setter functions rather than exposing the variable directly.\n318\t\n319\ttesting: |\n320\t  Every exported function and every meaningful branch deserves a test. Use\n321\t  table-driven parameterized tests for similar cases. The canonical structure is\n322\t  a slice of anonymous structs with a name field, run with t.Run:\n323\t\n324\t    tests := []struct {\n325\t      name  string\n326\t      input string\n327\t      want  int\n328\t    }{\n329\t      {\"empty\", \"\", 0},\n330\t      {\"one line\", \"hello\\n\", 1},\n331\t    }\n332\t    for _, tc := range tests {\n333\t      t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n334\t        got := countLines(tc.input)\n335\t        require.Equal(t, tc.want, got)\n336\t      })\n337\t    }\n338\t\n339\t  Call t.Parallel() as the first line of every test function that is safe to\n340\t  run concurrently. A test is safe to parallelize when it writes only to its own\n341\t  t.TempDir(), spawns isolated subprocesses, and does not call os.Chdir() or\n342\t  mutate package-level global state. Do NOT call t.Parallel() in tests that call\n343\t  os.Chdir() or any function that changes the process working directory — exec.Command\n344\t  calls without cmd.Dir observe the process cwd, so a parallel os.Chdir() will\n345\t  corrupt other tests running concurrently. In table-driven tests, add t.Parallel()\n346\t  inside each t.Run subtest when the rows are independent.\n347\t\n348\t  Call t.Helper() as the first line of every test helper function. This causes\n349\t  test failures to point to the caller site, not the helper internals:\n350\t\n351\t    func writeFile(t *testing.T, path, content string) {\n352\t      t.Helper()\n353\t      require.NoError(t, os.WriteFile(path, []byte(content), 0o644))\n354\t    }\n355\t\n356\t  Use TestMain(m *testing.M) when the package requires one-time setup — compiling\n357\t  a test binary, creating a shared fixture directory — that must complete before\n358\t  any test runs. Call os.Exit(m.Run()) at the end. Do not use TestMain for\n359\t  per-test setup; use t.Cleanup or helper functions for that.\n360\t\n361\t  Extract shared setup into helper functions. Build reusable test utilities in a\n362\t  testutil package. When writing tests, ask what inputs break assumptions: zero\n363\t  values, nil pointers, empty slices, duplicate keys, boundary lengths. If a bug\n364\t  could hide there, write a case for it.\n365\t\n366\tcode_review_checklist:\n367\t  - \"No duplicated logic exists that could be extracted into a shared function or struct.\"\n368\t  - \"No magic strings remain: all binaries, paths, and repeated text are centralized as named constants.\"\n369\t  - \"Every error is handled or explicitly discarded with a comment.\"\n370\t  - \"Every struct has a single, nameable responsibility.\"\n371\t  - \"No function exceeds 200 lines without a strong reason.\"\n372\t  - \"Every sub-package boundary has an interface. Interfaces are small (one to three methods).\"\n373\t  - \"No boolean parameters toggle behavior that should be a Strategy or Decorator.\"\n374\t  - \"Config structs embed shared fields rather than duplicating them.\"\n375\t  - \"context.Context is threaded through I/O paths.\"\n376\t  - \"Tests cover the contract, not the implementation.\"\n377\t  - \"Every test function that is safe to parallelize calls t.Parallel() as its first line.\"\n378\t  - \"No test calls t.Parallel() if it uses os.Chdir() or mutates package-level global state.\"\n379\t  - \"Every test helper calls t.Helper() as its first line.\"\n380\t  - \"All receiver names are one- or two-letter abbreviations, consistent across all methods of the type.\"\n381\t  - \"Imports are grouped: stdlib, external, internal — each group separated by a blank line.\"\n382\t  - \"Every exported symbol has a godoc comment starting with the symbol name.\"\n383\t  - \"No os.Getenv() call controls orchestrator behaviour, execution mode, file paths, or any option that belongs in configuration.yaml.\"\n384\t  - \"No init() function performs I/O or returns an error.\"\n385\t  - \"No panic() is reachable from a runtime condition or user-provided input.\"\n386\t  - \"Typed iota enums are used for any fixed set of integer values.\"\n387\t  - \"All source files are written before running build or test commands — no incremental compile-fix loops.\"\n388\t\n389\tsections:\n390\t  - tag: copyright_header\n391\t    title: Copyright Header\n392\t    content: |\n393\t      Every Go file must begin with the SPDX copyright header line.\n394\t  - tag: duplication\n395\t    title: Code Duplication\n396\t    content: |\n397\t      Search for existing code before writing a new function. Extract shared\n398\t      logic at the two-occurrence threshold — if you write the same thing twice,\n399\t      extract it.\n400\t  - tag: implementation_strategy\n401\t    title: Implementation Strategy\n402\t    content: |\n403\t      Write all source files before running any build or test commands. Plan\n404\t      the full implementation, write each file in dependency order, then\n405\t      compile and test once. This avoids incremental compile-fix loops that\n406\t      waste turns re-sending the entire prompt context.\n407\t  - tag: design_patterns\n408\t    title: Design Patterns\n409\t    content: |\n410\t      Eight patterns apply: Strategy, Command, Facade, Factory, Decorator,\n411\t      Builder, Adapter, and Observer. Select patterns to eliminate if/else ladders\n412\t      and centralize construction logic.\n413\t  - tag: interfaces\n414\t    title: Interfaces\n415\t    content: |\n416\t      Every boundary between a parent package and its internal sub-packages\n417\t      must be defined by an interface, even when only one implementation exists.\n418\t      Accept interfaces as parameters; return concrete structs. Keep interfaces\n419\t      small: one to three methods.\n420\t  - tag: struct_and_function_design\n421\t    title: Struct and Function Design\n422\t    content: |\n423\t      Each struct represents one concept; each function does one thing. Group\n424\t      more than three parameters into a config struct. Split functions exceeding\n425\t      200 lines at a natural seam.\n426\t  - tag: receiver_conventions\n427\t    title: Receiver Conventions\n428\t    content: |\n429\t      Use pointer receivers for stateful or large types, value receivers for small\n430\t      immutable types. All methods on a type use the same receiver kind. Name\n431\t      receivers with a one- or two-letter abbreviation of the type; never use\n432\t      self or this.\n433\t  - tag: error_handling\n434\t    title: Error Handling\n435\t    content: |\n436\t      Handle errors at the point they occur with guard clauses. Wrap errors with\n437\t      context using fmt.Errorf. Never silently discard an error except for\n438\t      best-effort cleanup, and always leave a comment when discarding.\n439\t  - tag: panic_vs_error\n440\t    title: Panic vs Error\n441\t    content: |\n442\t      Return errors for runtime conditions. Use panic only for programming\n443\t      contract violations. Never panic on I/O failures or user-provided input.\n444\t  - tag: init_functions\n445\t    title: init() Usage\n446\t    content: |\n447\t      Avoid init(). Use it only for side-effect registration (drivers, codecs)\n448\t      or infallible package-level lookup tables. Any initialization that can fail\n449\t      must use an explicit function called from main() or a constructor.\n450\t  - tag: no_magic_strings\n451\t    title: No Magic Strings\n452\t    content: |\n453\t      Centralize all string literals for binary names, file paths, URLs, and\n454\t      repeated text as named constants. Never scatter raw string literals.\n455\t  - tag: configuration_via_yaml\n456\t    title: Configuration via YAML Only\n457\t    content: |\n458\t      All configuration flows through configuration.yaml and the Config struct.\n459\t      Do not use os.Getenv() to control orchestrator behaviour, execution modes,\n460\t      or any option that belongs in configuration.yaml. os.Getenv() is\n461\t      permitted only for platform context (HOME, PATH) and, in test code, for\n462\t      variables the test harness itself sets.\n463\t\n464\t  - tag: constants\n465\t    title: Constants and Iota\n466\t    content: |\n467\t      Use typed constants for fixed sets of values. Use iota for sequential\n468\t      integer enums. Group related constants in a single const block. Keep\n469\t      constants unexported unless callers outside the package need them.\n470\t  - tag: project_structure\n471\t    title: Project Structure\n472\t    content: |\n473\t      cmd/ for entry points, internal/ for private implementation, pkg/ for\n474\t      public API surface, tests/ for integration tests, magefiles/ for build\n475\t      tooling. Every package organizes implementation into internal/\n476\t      sub-packages by domain concept. The parent package exports types and\n477\t      delegates; internal sub-packages provide the implementation.\n478\t  - tag: file_organization\n479\t    title: File Organization\n480\t    content: |\n481\t      Name files by concern, not by kind (no types.go, helpers.go, utils.go).\n482\t      Within a file: constants and types, then constructors, then exported\n483\t      symbols, then unexported helpers, then main(). Tests go in <file>_test.go.\n484\t  - tag: import_organization\n485\t    title: Import Organization\n486\t    content: |\n487\t      Three import groups separated by blank lines: stdlib, external modules,\n488\t      internal packages. No dot imports except by established test convention.\n489\t      No blank imports except for driver/codec side-effect registration.\n490\t  - tag: standard_packages\n491\t    title: Standard Packages\n492\t    content: |\n493\t      Approved dependencies: mage for build automation, cobra for CLI, viper for\n494\t      configuration, testify for test assertions, yaml.v3 for YAML parsing, and\n495\t      encoding/json from the standard library.\n496\t  - tag: struct_embedding\n497\t    title: Struct Embedding\n498\t    content: |\n499\t      Extract shared fields into a common struct and embed it. Provide\n500\t      registerXxxFlags helpers for embedded CLI flag groups.\n501\t  - tag: naming_conventions\n502\t    title: Naming Conventions\n503\t    content: |\n504\t      Exported names use PascalCase; unexported use camelCase. CLI flags use\n505\t      kebab-case. Binary constants use the binXxx prefix. Factories use NewXxx.\n506\t      Interfaces are named to describe behavior. Receivers use a one- or\n507\t      two-letter abbreviation of the type. Test helpers use verb names.\n508\t  - tag: comment_style\n509\t    title: Comment Style\n510\t    content: |\n511\t      Every exported symbol has a godoc comment starting with the symbol name.\n512\t      Unexported functions need comments only for non-obvious logic. Comments\n513\t      explain why, not what. Reference requirement IDs when implementing a\n514\t      specific requirement.\n515\t  - tag: concurrency\n516\t    title: Concurrency\n517\t    content: |\n518\t      Thread context.Context through all I/O paths. Protect package-level mutable\n519\t      state with sync.Mutex. Never start a goroutine without a plan for exit.\n520\t  - tag: testing\n521\t    title: Testing\n522\t    content: |\n523\t      Use table-driven tests with named struct rows and t.Run. Call t.Parallel()\n524\t      at the top of every test safe to run concurrently — but never in tests that\n525\t      call os.Chdir() or mutate global state. Call t.Helper() at the top of every\n526\t      test helper function. Use TestMain for one-time package setup. Tests cover\n527\t      the contract, not the implementation.\n528\t  - tag: code_review_checklist\n529\t    title: Code Review Checklist\n530\t    content: |\n531\t      Before closing: no duplicated logic, no magic strings, every error handled\n532\t      or discarded with a comment, single-responsibility structs, functions under\n533\t      200 lines, small interfaces, no boolean-toggle parameters, t.Parallel() on\n534\t      all safe tests, t.Helper() on all helpers, imports grouped, every exported\n535\t      symbol documented, no init() with I/O, no panic on runtime conditions,\n536\t      typed iota enums for fixed value sets.\n537\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CxWPahfEDXRg9WhSaGA74R","session_id":"e97c73f4-4027-474d-b337-bb9a89ad4527","uuid":"13df1bf6-12e2-4966-b40a-ac8b6d39b2a6","timestamp":"2026-05-27T15:33:51.964Z"}
